---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import ReferralCta from "../../components/ReferralCta.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, "");

const related = [
  {
    href: `${base}/guides/starlink-field-of-view-obstructions/`,
    title: "Field of view and obstructions, explained",
    description:
      "What the roughly 100° open-sky window means, elevation angles, and why the dish points away from the equator.",
  },
  {
    href: `${base}/guides/satellite-internet-for-remote-homes/`,
    title: "Satellite internet for remote homes",
    description:
      "How low-earth-orbit service works and why placement decides the experience at your address.",
  },
  {
    href: `${base}/planner/`,
    title: "The dish position map",
    description:
      "Drop a pin on satellite imagery and swing a 100° open-sky wedge clear of trees and rooflines.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Where to Place Your Starlink Dish — A Placement Guide"
  description="A practical walkthrough for choosing a Starlink dish placement: the open-sky window, pointing away from the equator, mount height, and comparing spots before you drill."
  path="/guides/where-to-place-starlink-dish/"
>
  <article class="article">
    <h1>Where to place your Starlink dish</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Choosing a dish location is the one part of Starlink installation you control before the
      hardware arrives. Get it right and the dish sees a clear patch of sky all day; get it wrong
      and trees and rooflines cause dropouts every time a satellite passes behind them.
    </p>

    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <p>
      Put the dish where it can see a clear, unobstructed window roughly
      <strong>100° wide</strong>, with that window opened <strong>away from the equator</strong> —
      north for homes in the United States and everywhere else in the northern hemisphere, south in
      the southern hemisphere. Everything below is detail; those two facts do most of the work.
    </p>

    <h2>Find the direction first</h2>
    <p>
      Starlink is a moving constellation: dozens of satellites cross your sky at any moment, packed
      densest toward the poleward side. The dish tracks them as they pass, so it doesn't aim at one
      point in the sky — it needs the whole corridor to stay open.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <h2>The TV-dish myth</h2>
      <p>
        Old advice says to point a dish south. That comes from geostationary TV satellites, which
        sit in a fixed belt over the equator — a completely different system. A Starlink dish in the
        US looks <strong>north</strong>, not south, and pointing it at the equator wastes the
        clearest part of the sky.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Walk your property like the dish sees it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Roof peak</strong> — usually the widest view, above most nearby clutter. Costs the
        most effort to reach and to cable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Eave or fascia</strong> — easier to mount and cable; works when the treeline sits
        below it on the poleward side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pole in the yard</strong> — often the cheapest way to buy height where roof access
        is awkward; keep it clear of the trees it's standing among.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Patio or ground mount</strong> — fine for testing a spot, rarely clear enough to
        live on, since even distant trees cut into the window.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Height matters, but <strong>a clear window matters more</strong>. A modest mount with open sky
      beats a tall one looking into a hedge. And remember foliage is seasonal: bare branches in
      March can be a wall of leaves in July.
    </p>

    <h2>Compare spots before you drill</h2>
    <p>
      Shortlist two or three candidate spots and compare them properly instead of eyeballing one.
      The <a href={`${base}/planner/`}>dish position map</a> draws the 100° open-sky wedge over
      satellite imagery of your property — drag the pin between spots and swing the wedge clear of
      trees and rooflines. Prefer a temporary mount first, confirm a day of trouble-free service,
      then commit to holes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once the hardware is in hand, the Starlink app's camera-based obstruction scan — run at the
      install spot — is the final word. The map planner gets you to that check with a placement
      that's already likely to pass.
    </p>
  </article>

  <RelatedLinks links={related} />

  <ReferralCta
    heading="Ready to order?"
    body="Once you know where the dish can live, check availability and sign up through Starlink's official site."
  />
</BaseLayout>
